
# OPF decommission strategy: what we retire, why, and in what order

Prepared 2026-10-01 for the director discussion. Snapshot of the program's plan as documented in the "OPF Wires Convergence Solution Options" deck (Sep 2026), ADR-0003 (Phase 1) and ADR-0004 (Phase 2). Both ADRs are still "Proposed", so details can move.

## The answer in five lines

1. **We decommission in two layers, not one.** First the Discover-owned systems around OPF (by May 17, 2027), then OPF itself (Q4 2027).
2. **Why two layers:** the May 17, 2027 mandate covers DFS-owned infrastructure only. OPF is hosted by FIS, a vendor, so it is exempt and can stay a little longer.
3. **Phase 1 (go-live April 2027):** wire origination and processing move into Capital One's PayX and OnePay. OPF is kept only as the gateway to the correspondent banks, reached by a new API. Every DFS-owned piece that touches OPF is retired.
4. **Phase 2 (go-live September 2027):** PayX connects to the correspondent banks directly, banks are cut over one at a time, the temporary PayX-to-OPF bridge is torn down, and the FIS contract is terminated in Q4 2027.
5. **Biggest risk is commercial:** the FIS contract must be extended to about Q4 2027 and that is not yet secured.

## Why we are decommissioning

- **Contractual deadline.** Under the Capital One and Discover deal, every DFS-owned application on DFS data centres or DFS cloud must be on Capital One ecosystems by **May 17, 2027**. Hard date.
- **Vendor exit.** The long-term target is zero dependency on OPF and FIS: all wires processed in PayX (the "Clearwater" end state), no ongoing vendor product overhead.
- **Why not do it all by May.** A single-step exit needs new connectivity and certification with four or more correspondent banks plus Treasury and Finance deliveries, all in one window with no slack. The deck calls it "too much risk to be done by May". Leadership asked the program to de-risk the date.
- **ISO 20022.** Specification updates are due Nov 13, 2027 (to be confirmed). The plan is to be off OPF before that deadline so message work is not done twice.

## Options considered

| | Option 1: full convergence by May 2027 | Option 2: PayX with interim OPF settlement (recommended and chosen) |
|---|---|---|
| Idea | Build everything in PayX, connect to banks directly, switch OPF off, all by May | Move only what the May mandate requires; keep OPF for core processing and bank integration; exit OPF by Q4 2027 |
| Pros | Target architecture in one step. No vendor overhead. | Retires the DFS-owned surrounds on time. No new bank connectivity needed for May. Higher confidence on both milestones. |
| Cons | Aggressive, high-risk timeline. New bank connectivity and exhaustive bank testing. Ops retrained on everything at once. | Ops run PayX initiation alongside OPF settlement for a while. Existing manual workarounds stay until the full exit. Needs continuous FIS engagement. |

