## Where our team (Pod 3) sits in this

- Pod 3, "Connect to OPF and Reports", builds the Phase 1 bridge: generate the pain.001 payment message, submit it to the FIS API, bring the outcome back. Plus reports to OneLake, VPN replacement and the Okta-to-Capital-One sign-on move.
- The bridge is deliberately transitional. It exists only because OPF is retained in Phase 1 and it is removed in step 5 above. The pain.001 generation itself survives and is extended in Phase 2 (pain.008, bank-specific variants).
- On the PI planning walkthrough, the architecture lead said the new ASV (the application container for this integration) is intended to be re-pointed from FIS to the correspondent banks in Phase 2 rather than thrown away, and retired only later when Capital One connects to Fed and SWIFT directly. Speaker attribution on that recording is not yet confirmed.

## Risks and open items the director should know

1. **FIS contract extension to about Q4 2027 is not secured.** Without it Phase 1 is not deliverable and the program is back to the single-step exit.
2. **The exemption for vendor-hosted systems is an assumption** that the ADR says should be confirmed in writing.
3. **Teardown is not costed anywhere.** The deck's Phase 2 slide has no teardown line item (ADR-0004 discrepancy D6, open).
4. **Step 6, data retention, is easy to miss.** Once the FIS contract ends, wire history held in OPF may be inaccessible. What to extract, in what format and where it lands must be decided before notice is served.
5. **Bank certification is externally paced.** "No staffing level compresses bank certification windows."
6. **CMOD to OneLake is still "In Discovery" with no named owner** on the deck.
7. **Okta and VPN disposition is unclear.** On the walkthrough the open questions were: is DFS Okta decommissioned by May, who drives it (enterprise or each application), and how OPF authorization works. Known so far: Okta evaluates the user's groups and passes them to OPF in the SAML token.
8. **Pending program decisions:** NCR data segregation, Capital One as settlement agent, Compliance Link contingency, DCI to Citi scope, whether wires keyed directly in bank portals are out of scope.
9. **Consumer (BPM) wires** are out of scope and move under the PIX program. If PIX slips past May 2027 there is no solution yet; it is logged as a risk.
10. **Phase 1 bridge blockers on our side:** the FIS API does not support drawdowns (250 to 350 a day); no field-level encryption to FIS (compliance call 2026-10-02); the status-message path (pain.002 over MQ) has no link and no owner; Code Connect access needs a Discover id.
11. **Fallback if OnePay is late:** keep using PO in OPF for manual initiation (about 80 wires a day) with manual OFAC screening.
12. **Governance:** all four ADRs are "Proposed", none ratified by the Architecture Review Board. ADR-0003 review is due by 2026-10-31.

